**What this plan does not cover (stated plainly):**

- **The trigger cannot be rolled back.** The Capacity Block reclaim was an external, scheduled AWS event; both reservations (cr-0013d27d3b3d5dc3b, cr-0884d02f8b1b344e5) are already deleted. This is a forward-looking restore, not a revert.
- **Capacity availability is not guaranteed.** Acquiring a new B200 Capacity Block depends on AWS availability at plan-execution time; if none exists, the fallback (On-Demand Capacity Reservation or on-demand instances) is documented but may carry different cost/availability.
- **Application/job owner action required.** The 2-node remnant was near-idle (GPUPowerUtilization peak ~0.021%) and FSx was barely read (~13 B/s) — the multi-node training job most likely could not make progress at reduced scale. After capacity is restored, the job owner must resume the job from its last checkpoint at full fleet size; this plan restores infrastructure, not job state.
- **FSx for Lustre fs-077c776983688ad76 is intentionally left untouched** — it was ruled healthy and is not part of the cause.
- The AMP collector and CloudWatch-agent repairs require access to the head node / collection topology that I could not fully enumerate from the control plane; those steps are given as operator actions to adapt to the actual configuration.

